  <h1>FAQ — Common Questions and Body Responses</h1>

  <h2>Getting Started</h2>

  <h3>Is Zhan Zhuang really effective? It looks like just standing still.</h3>
  <p>Professor Yu Yongnian explained in the BBC documentary: although Zhan Zhuang appears motionless on the outside, it actively rests the cerebral cortex through mental inhibition, improves microcirculation through subtle postural adjustments, boosts metabolism, and strengthens the immune system. Many long-term practitioners report stress relief, increased energy, and improved physical constitution. Even if you are skeptical, you should try it yourself to experience the changes.</p>

  <h3>How long should I practice each day?</h3>
  <p>Beginners start with 3–5 minutes and gradually increase. The goal is to comfortably hold each posture for 20 minutes.
  Consistent short daily practice is more effective than occasional long sessions.</p>

  <h3>What is the best time to practice?</h3>
  <p>Morning upon waking or before bed are ideal, but any time works. The key is to practice in a quiet, well-ventilated environment, and avoid practicing immediately after meals.</p>

  <h3>Do I need special equipment?</h3>
  <p>No. Wear comfortable, loose-fitting clothing that allows free movement. You can practice barefoot or in flat-soled shoes — your feet should be able to feel the ground.</p>

  <h2>Body Responses</h2>

  <h3>Is it normal for my legs to ache and shake during practice?</h3>
  <p>Yes. This is your muscles adapting to a new load. It is very common in the beginning and will gradually disappear with continued practice. If it becomes truly unbearable, you can stand up a bit to rest, then continue.</p>

  <h3>What does tingling, prickling, or warmth in the fingers mean?</h3>
  <p>This is a <strong>good sign of chi (energy) flow</strong>. Master Lam has mentioned many times that prickling, warmth, or a feeling of swelling in the fingers all indicate the practice is working.</p>

  <h3>Is sweating during practice normal?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Even without external movement, internal energy is at work, and sweating is a common response.</p>

  <h3>My body sways involuntarily — is that normal?</h3>
  <p>This is "Internal Movement" (Nei Dong), a normal phenomenon in Zhan Zhuang. The body produces subtle internal movements while outwardly still. Do not try to deliberately control it, nor deliberately amplify it.</p>

  <h3>What should I do if I feel dizzy or overheated?</h3>
  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <p><strong>Stop immediately</strong>, perform the cool-down movements, then sit down and rest. Do not push through it.
    This likely means your practice time is too long or the intensity is too high — reduce the duration.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Practice Questions</h2>

  <h3>When can I move on to the next posture?</h3>
  <p>When you can <strong>comfortably hold the current posture for about 20 minutes</strong>, you can move on to the next posture.
  Each posture builds on the previous one — do not skip stages.</p>

  <h3>Can I practice multiple postures in one session?</h3>
  <p>Yes. After mastering a posture, you can begin with 5 minutes of the previous posture as a warm-up, then transition to the new posture. The Five-Posture Flow is the practice of all five postures in a single session.</p>

  <h3>What if my family thinks practicing Zhan Zhuang is strange?</h3>
  <p>Several students in the BBC documentary mentioned this social pressure. It is normal.
  Zhan Zhuang is not widely known in the West, but the long-term benefits far outweigh the initial awkwardness.
  You can practice privately, or in inconspicuous settings like waiting for a bus.</p>

  <h3>Is it okay to practice intermittently?</h3>
  <p>Student Jim emphasized in Day 4: if you practice on and off, your body keeps reverting to the starting state.
  <strong>Daily consistency</strong> is the most important thing.</p>

  <h2>Safety</h2>

  <h3>Who should not practice Zhan Zhuang?</h3>
  <p>People with cardiovascular disease, orthostatic hypotension, severe joint conditions, or who are pregnant should consult a doctor before deciding.
  Although Zhan Zhuang is low-intensity, the static postures may cause blood pressure changes or joint discomfort.</p>

  <h3>What should I do about knee pain?</h3>
  <p>Make sure your knees do not extend past your toes. If knee pain persists, reduce the depth of your knee bend and practice time, and consult a doctor if necessary.</p>
